  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over northeast\nColorado/western Nebraska with inverted trough stretching\nthrough central South Dakota into south central North Dakota,\nwhile high remains over Alberta into Saskatchewan. Upper level\nanalysis places broad high over the southwestern CONUS with\nmodest ridging to the north, while low remains over northeast\nManitoba/northern Ontario. Over our area, convection remains to\nthe south and east of the James River Valley ahead of the\naforementioned surface trough. \n\nFor today, quiet weather starts the day as aforementioned\ninverted trough pushes off to the east. As we get into the\nafternoon and evening hours, another surface low deepens and\npushes through northern Wyoming into southwest South Dakota and\nwestern Wyoming, while an inverted trough pushes into northwest\nSouth Dakota and western North Dakota. In the meantime, a pair\nof modest short waves slide through west/northwest flow into our\narea. This will bring the next chances for showers/thunderstorms\nto western and central North Dakota late today into tonight. In\nthe meantime, elevated deep layer shear is noted during this \nperiod, with 0-6km values around 45-55kts. The question is how \nmuch moisture can be advected in ahead of the inverted trough, \nand thus how elevated the instability may be. With that said, \ngiven the strong shear and the elements present to produce \nconvection, the severe threat will be present going through the\nevening. \n\nWest-northwest flow pattern remains active our our area through\nmid-week bringing a daily chance for showers and thunderstorms,\nwith clusters suggesting an upper flow shift to more westerly \nor even a bit southwesterly as we get later in the week, which \nwould continue precipitation chances. Temperatures remain near \nto a bit below average through mid-week, cooling below average \nfor later in the week.",
